survived.mechanism:
`get_runtime_path_block_report` seeds and propagates the access-reachability set over `queue_room_ids` only:

    accessible_room_ids = {
        room_id
        for room_id in queue_room_ids
        if not requires_map.get(room_id)
    }

and the fixpoint loop filters parents to the queue as well (1628-1632):

    parent_ids = [
        parent_id
        for parent_id in requires_map.get(room_id, [])
        if parent_id in queue_room_id_set
    ]

Its declared sibling site `_build_effective_start_plan` does the SAME walk over ALL rooms (1132-1136, 1145):

    accessible_room_ids = {room_id for room_id in all_rooms_by_id if not requires_map.get(room_id)}
    ...
    parent_ids = list(requires_map.get(room_id, []))

`requires_map` is built by `rooms/access_graph.py:_build_room_access_views` over every managed room (access_graph.py:251-274), and `multiple_inbound` is a STRUCTURAL issue, so each room has at most one parent. Therefore a queued room whose single access parent is not itself queued ("Hallway grants access to Bedroom"; user runs Bedroom only) can never enter `accessible_room_ids`: it is not seeded (requires_map is non-empty) and `if parent_ids and any(...)` is False because parent_ids filters to []. It falls through to the affected-rooms loop at 1642-1672 and is emitted as `source: "access_dependency", reason: "access_blocked", blocked_by_room_id: None`.

The direct-rule arm is gated on `self._manager._room_rule_matches(rule)` (1584), but the access-dependency arm above is NOT — so the phantom block is produced on ANY watched blocker-entity state change, including a blocker turning OFF. `listeners/path_blockers.py:116` only skips when `old_state == new_state`, then at 139-157 applies the job's `path_block_action`: `cancel_and_event` calls `async_cancel_active_job` and fires EVENT_JOB_FINISHED + EVENT_RUN_INCOMPLETE; `pause_and_event` pauses the running robot. `path_block_action` is a first-class user-selectable option (`services.yaml:842`, `services/job_control.py:83,103`, default `event_only` at `jobs/active_job.py:289`).

    is_custom = selected_normalized in {"", "custom", "user_1"} or (
        selected_normalized != resolved_normalized
        and selected_normalized not in {"vacuum_quick", "vacuum_deep", "vacuum_mop_quick", "vacuum_mop_deep"}
    )

The second disjunct exists to flag "a constraint rewrote the profile". The ONLY function that makes selected != resolved is `resolve_profile_name_for_constraints` (room_profiles.py:342-369), and it rewrites exactly three names: `vacuum_mop_quick`, `vacuum_mop_deep`, `vacuum_mop_standard`. The first two are members of the hardcoded exclusion set, so the `and` kills the disjunct. The third is unreachable: `_normalize_profile_name` is called at the top of the same function (line 355) and LEGACY_PROFILE_ALIASES already folds `vacuum_mop_standard -> vacuum_mop_quick` (room_profiles.py:135-138), so the `if normalized_name == "vacuum_mop_standard"` branch at line 366 can never be reached either. The divergence arm therefore never evaluates True. Consequence at run_plan.py:130-131: `if not is_custom and selected_profile_label:` picks the SELECTED label, so a carpet room carrying `vacuum_mop_quick` (resolved down to `vacuum_quick`) renders `profile_label = "<Room> Vacuum + Mop Quick"`. Reached live through `core/manager.py:build_room_payload` (2484-2530), which passes rooms through `protected_room_config` only and preserves the STORED profile_name, then `get_payload_state` (2554-2581) labels them. The subtitle cannot compensate: `clean_mode` is not in `subtitle_bits`, and carpet water is "Off", which line 142 explicitly suppresses. The exclusion set is also a hand-copied duplicate of `BUILT_IN_ROOM_PROFILES.keys()` and of `presetCodes` in src/renderers/metrics.js.

    table = rate_override if (isinstance(rate_override, dict) and rate_override) else {"off": 0.0}
    return _safe_float(table.get(normalized, 4.0), 4.0)

The adapter's table does not merge over the core's `{"off": 0.0}` — it replaces it. `adapters/config_schema.py:1577-1586` declares `water_rates` as `required: False` with the description "Omit to use the generic table", enumerating the keys as guidance rather than a contract; nothing validates that "off" is present. An adapter declaring `{"low": 3.2, "medium": 4.0, "high": 5.3}` therefore makes `table.get("off", 4.0)` return 4.0. That directly falsifies the load-bearing comment in `estimate_job_water_usage` at run_plan.py:383-388: "_water_rate_ml_per_minute already returns 0 ml/min for water_level=off, so robot-water accounting stays self-correcting" — which is the stated reason the estimator gates on clean_mode instead of water_level. The same fallback also silently bills 4.0 ml/min for any level a brand's table omits (e.g. a five-level device). Eufy's own table (adapters/eufy/water_config.py:33) happens to include "off": 0.0, so it is safe today; Roborock declares no water_model_configs at all and correctly reports model_unsupported.

    room_entry.update(
        _room_surface_labels(
            floor_type=room.get("floor_type"),
        )
    )

`room` here is an element of `resolved_rooms`, built by `queue_engine.build_room_clean_payload` (queue_engine.py:314-342). That record deliberately carries `"carpet": str(resolved.get("floor_type","")).startswith("carpet")` — a boolean — and NO `floor_type` key. `_room_surface_labels` (run_plan.py:167-175) therefore takes the `if floor_value else None` branch every time and emits `floor_type_label: None`. The identical call in `core/manager.py:2575-2579` (get_payload_state) has the same input and the same result. `resolve_room_profile_for_room` does return `floor_type` in EffectiveRoomSettings (room_profiles.py:461); the payload builder is where it is dropped.
